# Charts and data visualisation (CH)

This is the short form. Before writing code in this area, read the full rules with reasons, bad/good examples and checks: `.ui-polish/rules/18-charts.md`.

- **CH-01 The chart answers one question** (warning). Pick the form from the question: change over time is a line (or columns for few periods); comparing categories is a horizontal bar sorted by value; part of a whole with two or three parts is a single stacked bar or a donut with labels; distribution is a histogram. No 3D, no pie with more than five slices, no dual y-axes, no radar for comparison. If a reader needs exact values, show a table (DT-01) or put the numbers on the chart. Give each chart a title that states the takeaway ("Signups doubled after the March launch").
- **CH-02 Colour comes from chart tokens and means something** (warning). Series colours come from the chart tokens (CO-05), defined for light and dark, never from the library default. One series is the focus: give it `--chart-1` and the rest a neutral or lighter tone. The same category keeps the same colour on every chart of the product. Use at most six categorical colours; group the rest as "Other". Grid lines and axes use `--border` and `--muted-foreground` and stay quieter than the data.
- **CH-03 Label directly; axes have units** (warning). Put series names at the end of their line or on the bar instead of in a distant legend; keep a legend only for more than four series, placed above the chart. Axes show units and readable ticks formatted with `Intl.NumberFormat` (`12k`, `$1.2M`, `Mar 3`), at most about six ticks, no rotated labels (switch to horizontal bars instead). Show values on bars when there are fewer than about twelve.
- **CH-04 Scales are honest** (error). Bar and area charts start at zero. Line charts may zoom in, but say so and never compare two zoomed charts side by side with different scales. Small multiples share one scale. Percentages of a whole add up to 100. Time axes are evenly spaced in time, with gaps shown as gaps, not joined.
- **CH-05 Readable without colour or sight** (error). Series differ by more than hue: direct labels, dash patterns or marker shapes. Each chart has a text alternative: an accessible name and a one- or two-sentence summary of what it shows (`role="img"` plus `aria-label` on the SVG, or a visible caption), and the underlying numbers are reachable as a table or download. Interactive points are keyboard reachable, and tooltips also appear on focus (AX-10). Text in charts meets contrast (AX-09); lines and bars meet 3:1 against the background.
- **CH-06 Charts have states and fit their box** (warning). Reserve the chart's height before data arrives and show a skeleton of the same size (ST-02). Empty data says what is missing for which range ("No orders between 1 and 7 March") instead of drawing a flat line at zero. Errors appear in the chart's box with a retry. On narrow containers, reduce ticks and series rather than shrinking text; use the container's width, not the viewport (RS-09).
- **CH-07 Interaction adds detail, not decoration** (info). Tooltips show the formatted value, unit and series name, appear on hover and focus, and follow the theme tokens. Hover highlights the hovered series and fades the rest. Entry animation is short (under 400ms), runs once, and is off under `prefers-reduced-motion` (MO-03). Big-number KPI tiles show the comparison that gives the number meaning ("+12% vs last month") with an arrow and text, not colour alone.
